From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 15:38:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Phase 6, first batch: real schema slots for 4 hardcoded
 thresholds/margins found during the 34-worker audit
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Four fixes, all previously flagged in this codebase's own comments as
"needs a schema migration, tracked as Phase 6" — this batch closes them.
Grouped in one commit because 2 of the 4 share a single drizzle-kit
generate pass (migration 0053_married_korath.sql), and splitting that
migration artificially would be less honest than committing it as the
one atomic unit it actually is.

1. Leak-Map's AGING_THRESHOLD_DAYS (audit-engine.ts) — was a bare
   hardcoded local const (30) with no schema column at all. Now
   EngagementStack.aging_threshold_days (schema.ts, no migration needed —
   stack is jsonb), same default preserved, tiered hidden-default in
   worker-registry.ts (real default, never blocks) and wired into
   filledKeysForLeakMap.

2. whop-refund-dispute-velocity's 3 thresholds
   (refund-dispute-velocity-service.ts) — REFUND_RATE_THRESHOLD (0.08),
   DISPUTE_ALERT_THRESHOLD (3), MIN_SAMPLE_SIZE (10) were all hardcoded
   module-level consts. Now EngagementStack.refund_dispute_rate_threshold /
   dispute_alert_threshold / min_payment_sample_size (no migration —
   jsonb), same defaults preserved. Named refund_dispute_rate_threshold
   (not refund_rate_threshold) because the service compares BOTH refund
   rate and dispute rate against this one value today — the field name
   honestly reflects that shared behavior rather than implying a distinct
   override that doesn't exist in the code.
   RECONCILIATION_COOLDOWN_HOURS deliberately stays hardcoded — an
   internal alert-spam guard, not a business threshold. Added 3 fields to
   WHOP_AGENT_CONFIG_FIELDS["whop-refund-dispute-velocity"], matching the
   untiered convention that whole map already uses (no UI form exists;
   hasHingesPanel stays false, same "real schema slot, no setter yet"
   treatment already established for win-back's recoveryWindowDays).

3. send-report's REPORT_WINDOW_DAYS (send-report.ts) — was hardcoded,
   AND independently duplicated a second time in
   cold-open-findings/route.ts (silently divergeable from send-report's
   own copy). Now coldOpenConfig.reportWindowDays (schema.ts) — a real
   migration, since coldOpenConfig is a typed table, not a jsonb blob.
   Both call sites now read the same column; cold-open-findings/route.ts
   restructured to fetch config before computing `since` instead of
   after (it was previously computed from the hardcoded constant inside
   the same Promise.all as the config fetch it now depends on). Added a
   real WorkerConfigField entry (was previously an empty array with a
   comment explaining exactly this gap), tiered hidden-default.

4. Pre-Call-Read's identity-match margin (person-match.ts /
   brief-service.ts) — evaluatePersonMatch already computed a per-signal
   `trace` (domain/name/LinkedIn/company points, total, threshold) but
   only `totalScore` was ever persisted; the margin explanation was
   discarded the moment each run finished. Added
   briefedCallsLog.personMatchTrace (schema.ts, real migration) and wired
   it into all 3 write sites that already persist personMatchScore
   (matching the existing 4th site, the failure-path onConflictDoUpdate,
   which deliberately omits personMatchScore too — same omission
   preserved for personMatchTrace). Surfaced in pre-call-read-view.tsx as
   an explicit "why?" toggle next to the Prospect Identity label, not
   shown by default — matching this same file's own established finding
   (Horror Story #2) that a raw Rule-14 score reads as a data-quality
   problem to someone who doesn't know what Rule 14 is; this is for
   whoever needs to actually debug a skip, not the default glance.

Migration 0053_married_korath.sql: two additive, nullable/defaulted
columns only (briefed_calls_log.person_match_trace jsonb,
cold_open_config.report_window_days integer default 7 not null) — no
drops, no destructive changes. Generated via `npx drizzle-kit generate`,
which diffs against the migration journal, not a live DB connection
(none available in this sandbox) — not run/applied here.

Verification: npx tsc --noEmit stayed at the established baseline of 5
pre-existing errors in unrelated test files. `next build`'s compile and
TypeScript phases both pass cleanly; its page-data collection step still
fails in this sandbox on DATABASE_URL not being set, the same
pre-existing sandbox limitation confirmed throughout Phase 5. npx vitest
run stayed at the established baseline: 59 failed / 191 passed / 18
skipped, unchanged.

